  "answer_text": "**Yes, Coca-Cola did originally contain cocaine.** \n\nHowever, the history of how much was in the drink, why it was removed, and the surprising way Coca-Cola still relies on the coca plant today is a fascinating story.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Origin and the Name (1885)\nCoca-Cola was invented in 1885 by John Pemberton, an Atlanta pharmacist who was also a morphine addict trying to find a cure for his own addiction. He formulated the drink as a \"patent medicine\" and marketed it as a brain tonic that could cure headaches, fatigue, and depression. \n\nThe name **\"Coca-Cola\"** was derived from its two main active ingredients:\n* **\"Coca\"** from the *coca leaf*, which is the source of cocaine.\n* **\"Cola\"** from the *kola nut*, which is a natural source of caffeine.\n\n### 2. How Much Cocaine Was in It?\nIn the late 19th century, cocaine was completely legal and widely viewed as a harmless wonder-drug. Pemberton\u2019s original recipe called for about five ounces of coca leaf per gallon of syrup. \n\nThis translated to an estimated **9 milligrams of cocaine per glass**. To put that into perspective, a typical modern recreational dose (a single \"line\") of cocaine is roughly 50 to 75 milligrams. While a glass of early Coca-Cola would not have made someone wildly high, the combination of cocaine, caffeine, and sugar certainly gave drinkers a noticeable \"kick\" or buzz.\n\n### 3. When Was It Removed?\nAs the addictive nature of cocaine became clear and public opinion began to turn against it in the late 1890s, the company's owners realized they had a public relations problem. \n\n* **In 1903,** Coca-Cola officially removed active cocaine from the recipe. \n* Instead of using fresh coca leaves, they began using \"spent\" (decocainized) leaves for flavoring.\n* By **1929**, scientists perfected the extraction process, ensuring the beverage was entirely free of active cocaine molecules.\n\n### 4. The Modern Twist: Coca-Cola and Cocaine Today\nIncredibly, **Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves to flavor the drink today.** \n\nSince the flavor of the coca leaf is an essential part of Coke\u2019s secret formula, the company has a highly secure, government-sanctioned setup:\n* A specialty chemical plant in Maywood, New Jersey, owned by the **Stepan Company**, is the **only commercial entity in the United States authorized by the DEA to import and process coca leaves** (which they source from Peru and Bolivia).\n* Stepan Company processes about 100 metric tons of coca leaves each year. They extract the active cocaine from the leaves, leaving behind a decocainized extract.\n* The **cocaine-free extract** is sent to Coca-Cola to flavor the soda.\n* The **pure cocaine** that is extracted isn't destroyed; Stepan sells it to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a medical-grade topical anesthetic legally used by doctors for ear, nose, and throat surgeries.",
